<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\overline{g}$ be a homotopy inverse to $p(f)$ in $\calD $, so that there exists a $2$-simplex of $\calD $ depicted as follows: 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { &amp;  p(Y) \ar [dr]^{\overline{g}} &amp;  \\ p(X) \ar [ur]^{p(f)} \ar [rr]^{\id _{p(X)}} &amp;  &amp;  p(X)} \]
</div>
    <p>
       Since $p$ is a left fibration, we can lift this to a diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { &amp;  Y \ar [dr]^{g} &amp;  \\ X \ar [ur]^{f} \ar [rr]^{\id _{X}} &amp;  &amp;  X } \]
</div>
    <p>
       in $\calC $. It follows that $g \circ f \simeq \id _{X}$, so that $f$ admits a left homotopy inverse. Since $p(g) = \overline{g}$ is an equivalence in $\calD $, the same argument proves that $g$ has a left homotopy inverse. This left homotopy inverse must coincide with $f$, since $f$ is a right homotopy inverse to $g$. Thus $f$ and $g$ are homotopy inverse in the $\infty $-category $\calC $, so that $f$ is an equivalence as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>